\r\\n\" (CRLF). This is a problem for any Windows file that is being read either on a Unix-like platform or on Windows in binary mode. If the code already takes care of removing any trailing '\\r' after reading the line, add a '(* nosemgrep *)' comment to disable this warning.","text":"'input_line' leaves a '\\r' (CR) character when reading lines from a Windows text file, whose lines end in \"\\r\\n\" (CRLF). This is a problem for any Windows file that is being read either on a Unix-like platform or on Windows in binary mode. If the code already takes care of removing any trailing '\\r' after reading the line, add a '(* nosemgrep *)' comment to disable this warning."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.broken-input-line","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.broken-input-line","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.broken-input-line"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"You should probably use Filename.get_temp_dirname()."},"help":{"markdown":"You should probably use Filename.get_temp_dirname().","text":"You should probably use Filename.get_temp_dirname()."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.not-portable-tmp-string","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.not-portable-tmp-string","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.not-portable-tmp-string"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"'open_in' behaves differently on Windows and on Unix-like systems with respect to line endings. To get the same behavior everywhere, use 'open_in_bin' or 'open_in_gen [Open_binary]'. If you really want CRLF-to-LF translations to take place when running on Windows, use 'open_in_gen [Open_text]'."},"help":{"markdown":"'open_in' behaves differently on Windows and on Unix-like systems with respect to line endings. To get the same behavior everywhere, use 'open_in_bin' or 'open_in_gen [Open_binary]'. If you really want CRLF-to-LF translations to take place when running on Windows, use 'open_in_gen [Open_text]'.","text":"'open_in' behaves differently on Windows and on Unix-like systems with respect to line endings. To get the same behavior everywhere, use 'open_in_bin' or 'open_in_gen [Open_binary]'. If you really want CRLF-to-LF translations to take place when running on Windows, use 'open_in_gen [Open_text]'."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.prefer-read-in-binary-mode","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.prefer-read-in-binary-mode","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.prefer-read-in-binary-mode"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"'open_out' behaves differently on Windows and on Unix-like systems with respect to line endings. To get the same behavior everywhere, use 'open_out_bin' or 'open_out_gen [Open_binary]'. If you really want LF-to-CRLF translations to take place when running on Windows, use 'open_out_gen [Open_text]'."},"help":{"markdown":"'open_out' behaves differently on Windows and on Unix-like systems with respect to line endings. To get the same behavior everywhere, use 'open_out_bin' or 'open_out_gen [Open_binary]'. If you really want LF-to-CRLF translations to take place when running on Windows, use 'open_out_gen [Open_text]'.","text":"'open_out' behaves differently on Windows and on Unix-like systems with respect to line endings. To get the same behavior everywhere, use 'open_out_bin' or 'open_out_gen [Open_binary]'. If you really want LF-to-CRLF translations to take place when running on Windows, use 'open_out_gen [Open_text]'."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.prefer-write-in-binary-mode","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.prefer-write-in-binary-mode","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.portability.prefer-write-in-binary-mode"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Digest uses MD5 and should not be used for security purposes. Consider using SHA256 instead."},"help":{"markdown":"Digest uses MD5 and should not be used for security purposes. Consider using SHA256 instead.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Digest.html](https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Digest.html)\n","text":"Digest uses MD5 and should not be used for security purposes. Consider using SHA256 instead."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-digest","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-digest","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-328: Use of Weak Hash (4.12)","LOW CONFIDENCE","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-digest"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Executing external programs might lead to comand or argument injection vulnerabilities."},"help":{"markdown":"Executing external programs might lead to comand or argument injection vulnerabilities.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Unix.html](https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Unix.html)\n","text":"Executing external programs might lead to comand or argument injection vulnerabilities."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-exec","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-exec","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-78: OS Command Injection","LOW CONFIDENCE","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-exec"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"When attacker supplied data is passed to Filename.concat directory traversal attacks might be possible."},"help":{"markdown":"When attacker supplied data is passed to Filename.concat directory traversal attacks might be possible.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Filename.html](https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Filename.html)\n","text":"When attacker supplied data is passed to Filename.concat directory traversal attacks might be possible."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-filenameconcat","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-filenameconcat","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-35: Path Traversal","LOW CONFIDENCE","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-filenameconcat"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Creating a Hashtbl without the optional random number parameter makes it prone to DoS attacks when attackers are able to fill the table with malicious content. Hashtbl.randomize or the R flag in the OCAMLRUNPARAM are other ways to randomize it."},"help":{"markdown":"Creating a Hashtbl without the optional random number parameter makes it prone to DoS attacks when attackers are able to fill the table with malicious content. Hashtbl.randomize or the R flag in the OCAMLRUNPARAM are other ways to randomize it.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Hashtbl.html](https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Hashtbl.html)\n","text":"Creating a Hashtbl without the optional random number parameter makes it prone to DoS attacks when attackers are able to fill the table with malicious content. Hashtbl.randomize or the R flag in the OCAMLRUNPARAM are other ways to randomize it."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-hashtable-dos","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-hashtable-dos","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-399: Resource Management Errors (4.12)","LOW CONFIDENCE","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-hashtable-dos"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Marshaling is currently not type-safe and can lead to insecure behaviour when untrusted data is marshalled. Marshalling can lead to out-of-bound reads as well."},"help":{"markdown":"Marshaling is currently not type-safe and can lead to insecure behaviour when untrusted data is marshalled. Marshalling can lead to out-of-bound reads as well.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://eternal.red/2021/secure-ocaml-sandbox/](https://eternal.red/2021/secure-ocaml-sandbox/)\n","text":"Marshaling is currently not type-safe and can lead to insecure behaviour when untrusted data is marshalled. Marshalling can lead to out-of-bound reads as well."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-marshal","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-marshal","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data","LOW CONFIDENCE","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-marshal"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Filename.temp_file might lead to race conditions, since the file could be altered or replaced by a symlink before being opened."},"help":{"markdown":"Filename.temp_file might lead to race conditions, since the file could be altered or replaced by a symlink before being opened.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Filename.html](https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Filename.html)\n","text":"Filename.temp_file might lead to race conditions, since the file could be altered or replaced by a symlink before being opened."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-tempfile","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-tempfile","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-367: Time-of-check Time-of-use (TOCTOU) Race Condition","LOW CONFIDENCE","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-tempfile"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Unsafe functions do not perform boundary checks or have other side effects, use with care."},"help":{"markdown":"Unsafe functions do not perform boundary checks or have other side effects, use with care.\n\n<b>References:</b>\n - [https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Bigarray.Array1.html#VALunsafe_get](https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Bigarray.Array1.html#VALunsafe_get)\n - [https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Bytes.html#VALunsafe_to_string](https://v2.ocaml.org/api/Bytes.html#VALunsafe_to_string)\n","text":"Unsafe functions do not perform boundary checks or have other side effects, use with care."},"id":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-unsafe","name":"home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-unsafe","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":["CWE-242: Use of Inherently Dangerous Function (4.12)","MEDIUM CONFIDENCE","security"]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: home.jm.data.qnx..opengrep-rules.semgrep-rules.ocaml.lang.security.ocamllint-unsafe"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"error"},"fullDescription":{"text":"shm_open → mmap without an fstat size check between them. If the creator sized the object differently (code skew, partial create, or a new generation under the same name), mmap succeeds and the first touch past st_size delivers SIGBUS. fstat and require st_size >= expected bytes before mapping; on mismatch unmap/retry or fail cleanly.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"shm_open → mmap without an fstat size check between them. If the creator sized the object differently (code skew, partial create, or a new generation under the same name), mmap succeeds and the first touch past st_size delivers SIGBUS. fstat and require st_size >= expected bytes before mapping; on mismatch unmap/retry or fail cleanly.\n","text":"shm_open → mmap without an fstat size check between them. If the creator sized the object differently (code skew, partial create, or a new generation under the same name), mmap succeeds and the first touch past st_size delivers SIGBUS. fstat and require st_size >= expected bytes before mapping; on mismatch unmap/retry or fail cleanly.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-attach-without-fstat-size","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-attach-without-fstat-size","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-attach-without-fstat-size"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"error"},"fullDescription":{"text":"A potentially blocking QNX IPC call appears in a function that never calls caml_release_runtime_system — every other OCaml domain/thread stops for the duration of the block. Wrap blocking Msg* calls in release/acquire (pointer values taken before releasing).\n"},"help":{"markdown":"A potentially blocking QNX IPC call appears in a function that never calls caml_release_runtime_system — every other OCaml domain/thread stops for the duration of the block. Wrap blocking Msg* calls in release/acquire (pointer values taken before releasing).\n","text":"A potentially blocking QNX IPC call appears in a function that never calls caml_release_runtime_system — every other OCaml domain/thread stops for the duration of the block. Wrap blocking Msg* calls in release/acquire (pointer values taken before releasing).\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-blocking-call-holds-runtime-lock","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-blocking-call-holds-runtime-lock","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-blocking-call-holds-runtime-lock"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"caml_copy_int64 boxes an int64 on the OCaml heap. In load_acq / fetch_add this fires on every ring push/pop and vclock tick — the C side of the \"zero OCaml heap allocation in steady state\" hole. Expose [@@unboxed] externals (int64_t in/out) so the stub returns a raw integer without allocating.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"caml_copy_int64 boxes an int64 on the OCaml heap. In load_acq / fetch_add this fires on every ring push/pop and vclock tick — the C side of the \"zero OCaml heap allocation in steady state\" hole. Expose [@@unboxed] externals (int64_t in/out) so the stub returns a raw integer without allocating.\n","text":"caml_copy_int64 boxes an int64 on the OCaml heap. In load_acq / fetch_add this fires on every ring push/pop and vclock tick — the C side of the \"zero OCaml heap allocation in steady state\" hole. Expose [@@unboxed] externals (int64_t in/out) so the stub returns a raw integer without allocating.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-caml-copy-int64-hot-path","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-caml-copy-int64-hot-path","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-caml-copy-int64-hot-path"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"error"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Non-atomic check-then-store on a shared region word. Another process can modify the word between the read and the store (lost update or value inflation). Vclock.merge races this way against the owning peer's atomic fetch_add tick. Use a CAS loop or an atomic max primitive; a bare load + conditional store_rel is not atomic RMW.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Non-atomic check-then-store on a shared region word. Another process can modify the word between the read and the store (lost update or value inflation). Vclock.merge races this way against the owning peer's atomic fetch_add tick. Use a CAS loop or an atomic max primitive; a bare load + conditional store_rel is not atomic RMW.\n","text":"Non-atomic check-then-store on a shared region word. Another process can modify the word between the read and the store (lost update or value inflation). Vclock.merge races this way against the owning peer's atomic fetch_add tick. Use a CAS loop or an atomic max primitive; a bare load + conditional store_rel is not atomic RMW.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-check-then-store-on-shared-word","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-check-then-store-on-shared-word","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-check-then-store-on-shared-word"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"ConnectAttach failure is reported only as caml_failwith(\"ConnectAttach\"), discarding errno. ESRCH/EINVAL here is the stale-session signal (dead producer pid/chid published in a previous generation). Return the error to OCaml so the caller can drop the mapping, munmap, and retry/remap against a fresh READY publication instead of crashing the joiner.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"ConnectAttach failure is reported only as caml_failwith(\"ConnectAttach\"), discarding errno. ESRCH/EINVAL here is the stale-session signal (dead producer pid/chid published in a previous generation). Return the error to OCaml so the caller can drop the mapping, munmap, and retry/remap against a fresh READY publication instead of crashing the joiner.\n","text":"ConnectAttach failure is reported only as caml_failwith(\"ConnectAttach\"), discarding errno. ESRCH/EINVAL here is the stale-session signal (dead producer pid/chid published in a previous generation). Return the error to OCaml so the caller can drop the mapping, munmap, and retry/remap against a fresh READY publication instead of crashing the joiner.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-connect-attach-discards-errno","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-connect-attach-discards-errno","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-connect-attach-discards-errno"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"caml_failwith with a fixed string discards errno (and QNX's error detail). Callers see \"MsgSendv\" / \"shm_open\" with no strerror, which makes EAGAIN vs EINTR vs EBADF indistinguishable — the pulse EAGAIN crash is the sharp edge of this pattern. Prefer caml_uerror / caml_unix_error, or include strerror(errno) in the message.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"caml_failwith with a fixed string discards errno (and QNX's error detail). Callers see \"MsgSendv\" / \"shm_open\" with no strerror, which makes EAGAIN vs EINTR vs EBADF indistinguishable — the pulse EAGAIN crash is the sharp edge of this pattern. Prefer caml_uerror / caml_unix_error, or include strerror(errno) in the message.\n","text":"caml_failwith with a fixed string discards errno (and QNX's error detail). Callers see \"MsgSendv\" / \"shm_open\" with no strerror, which makes EAGAIN vs EINTR vs EBADF indistinguishable — the pulse EAGAIN crash is the sharp edge of this pattern. Prefer caml_uerror / caml_unix_error, or include strerror(errno) in the message.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-failwith-discards-errno","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-failwith-discards-errno","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-failwith-discards-errno"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"failwith reachable at runtime: inventory the crash surface for steady-state operation. Region/Arena exhaustion are by design (preallocation contract violations), but a SCHED_FIFO/IPC failure path taking the process down may deserve a degraded-mode fallback.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"failwith reachable at runtime: inventory the crash surface for steady-state operation. Region/Arena exhaustion are by design (preallocation contract violations), but a SCHED_FIFO/IPC failure path taking the process down may deserve a degraded-mode fallback.\n","text":"failwith reachable at runtime: inventory the crash surface for steady-state operation. Region/Arena exhaustion are by design (preallocation contract violations), but a SCHED_FIFO/IPC failure path taking the process down may deserve a degraded-mode fallback.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-failwith-runtime-surface","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-failwith-runtime-surface","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-failwith-runtime-surface"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"error"},"fullDescription":{"text":"ftruncate on a shm_open fd with O_CREAT but without O_EXCL: if the shared object already exists (e.g. a previous run or another participant sized it differently), this silently resizes shared state other processes may be using — stale/partial mappings, SIGBUS, or silent data loss. Use O_EXCL for create, or fstat and verify the existing size before truncating.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"ftruncate on a shm_open fd with O_CREAT but without O_EXCL: if the shared object already exists (e.g. a previous run or another participant sized it differently), this silently resizes shared state other processes may be using — stale/partial mappings, SIGBUS, or silent data loss. Use O_EXCL for create, or fstat and verify the existing size before truncating.\n","text":"ftruncate on a shm_open fd with O_CREAT but without O_EXCL: if the shared object already exists (e.g. a previous run or another participant sized it differently), this silently resizes shared state other processes may be using — stale/partial mappings, SIGBUS, or silent data loss. Use O_EXCL for create, or fstat and verify the existing size before truncating.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-ftruncate-resizes-shared-object","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-ftruncate-resizes-shared-object","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-ftruncate-resizes-shared-object"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Int64 arithmetic returns a freshly boxed int64. On hot paths (ring push/pop staging, merkle mix/update, demo tight loops) each add/mul/ sub/logxor is a minor allocation on top of any of_int boxing. Prefer unboxed int64 externals or keep heavy mixers off the steady-state path if the zero-allocation claim must hold strictly.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Int64 arithmetic returns a freshly boxed int64. On hot paths (ring push/pop staging, merkle mix/update, demo tight loops) each add/mul/ sub/logxor is a minor allocation on top of any of_int boxing. Prefer unboxed int64 externals or keep heavy mixers off the steady-state path if the zero-allocation claim must hold strictly.\n","text":"Int64 arithmetic returns a freshly boxed int64. On hot paths (ring push/pop staging, merkle mix/update, demo tight loops) each add/mul/ sub/logxor is a minor allocation on top of any of_int boxing. Prefer unboxed int64 externals or keep heavy mixers off the steady-state path if the zero-allocation claim must hold strictly.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-int64-arith-hot-path","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-int64-arith-hot-path","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-int64-arith-hot-path"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Int64.of_int allocates a boxed int64 on the OCaml heap outside init/create. On push/pop/tick/free/seti (and demo steady-state loops) this contradicts the \"zero OCaml heap allocation in steady state\" contract. Keep boxing on init-only paths, use [@@unboxed] externals, or accept and document the minor-allocation rate.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Int64.of_int allocates a boxed int64 on the OCaml heap outside init/create. On push/pop/tick/free/seti (and demo steady-state loops) this contradicts the \"zero OCaml heap allocation in steady state\" contract. Keep boxing on init-only paths, use [@@unboxed] externals, or accept and document the minor-allocation rate.\n","text":"Int64.of_int allocates a boxed int64 on the OCaml heap outside init/create. On push/pop/tick/free/seti (and demo steady-state loops) this contradicts the \"zero OCaml heap allocation in steady state\" contract. Keep boxing on init-only paths, use [@@unboxed] externals, or accept and document the minor-allocation rate.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-int64-boxing-in-hot-path","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-int64-boxing-in-hot-path","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-int64-boxing-in-hot-path"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Int64.of_int boxes on an init/create path. Expected for one-time region setup (ring cap/width, vclock peer count, arena free-list chain). Not a steady-state concern; tracked separately from hot-path boxing so init noise does not hide push/pop allocations.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Int64.of_int boxes on an init/create path. Expected for one-time region setup (ring cap/width, vclock peer count, arena free-list chain). Not a steady-state concern; tracked separately from hot-path boxing so init noise does not hide push/pop allocations.\n","text":"Int64.of_int boxes on an init/create path. Expected for one-time region setup (ring cap/width, vclock peer count, arena free-list chain). Not a steady-state concern; tracked separately from hot-path boxing so init noise does not hide push/pop allocations.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-int64-boxing-in-init","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-int64-boxing-in-init","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-int64-boxing-in-init"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"MAP_FIXED silently replaces any existing mapping overlapping the requested range — a wrong hint address can destroy the process's own address space with no error. Prefer MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE (check EEXIST) or verify the range is free before mapping.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"MAP_FIXED silently replaces any existing mapping overlapping the requested range — a wrong hint address can destroy the process's own address space with no error. Prefer MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE (check EEXIST) or verify the range is free before mapping.\n","text":"MAP_FIXED silently replaces any existing mapping overlapping the requested range — a wrong hint address can destroy the process's own address space with no error. Prefer MAP_FIXED_NOREPLACE (check EEXIST) or verify the range is free before mapping.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-map-fixed-silent-overwrite","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-map-fixed-silent-overwrite","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-map-fixed-silent-overwrite"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"error"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Mapping byte length is computed as (size_t)Long_val(words) * 8 without an overflow check. A large words count wraps size_t, under-allocates the mmap, and later region stores walk off the mapping (SIGBUS / corruption). Reject words that would overflow or use a checked multiply before mmap/ftruncate.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Mapping byte length is computed as (size_t)Long_val(words) * 8 without an overflow check. A large words count wraps size_t, under-allocates the mmap, and later region stores walk off the mapping (SIGBUS / corruption). Reject words that would overflow or use a checked multiply before mmap/ftruncate.\n","text":"Mapping byte length is computed as (size_t)Long_val(words) * 8 without an overflow check. A large words count wraps size_t, under-allocates the mmap, and later region stores walk off the mapping (SIGBUS / corruption). Reject words that would overflow or use a checked multiply before mmap/ftruncate.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-map-size-overflow","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-map-size-overflow","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-map-size-overflow"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"mmap without a matching munmap in the same function. On the success path the Bigarray may own the mapping for the process lifetime, but any failure after a successful mmap (or a missing custom finalizer) leaks the virtual mapping. Pair error paths with munmap, and ensure GC finalization calls munmap when the region is released.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"mmap without a matching munmap in the same function. On the success path the Bigarray may own the mapping for the process lifetime, but any failure after a successful mmap (or a missing custom finalizer) leaks the virtual mapping. Pair error paths with munmap, and ensure GC finalization calls munmap when the region is released.\n","text":"mmap without a matching munmap in the same function. On the success path the Bigarray may own the mapping for the process lifetime, but any failure after a successful mmap (or a missing custom finalizer) leaks the virtual mapping. Pair error paths with munmap, and ensure GC finalization calls munmap when the region is released.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-mmap-without-munmap","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-mmap-without-munmap","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-mmap-without-munmap"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Modulo by t.cap: Ring.attach reads cap from the region with no validation, so attaching to a not-yet-initialized slice gives cap=0 and raises Division_by_zero at runtime instead of failing at attach. Validate cap > 0 (and width > 0) in attach.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Modulo by t.cap: Ring.attach reads cap from the region with no validation, so attaching to a not-yet-initialized slice gives cap=0 and raises Division_by_zero at runtime instead of failing at attach. Validate cap > 0 (and width > 0) in attach.\n","text":"Modulo by t.cap: Ring.attach reads cap from the region with no validation, so attaching to a not-yet-initialized slice gives cap=0 and raises Division_by_zero at runtime instead of failing at attach. Validate cap > 0 (and width > 0) in attach.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-mod-capacity-without-zero-guard","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-mod-capacity-without-zero-guard","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-mod-capacity-without-zero-guard"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"MsgSendv collects the reply into a stack-local buffer that is never read — the reply payload is silently discarded (the comment suggests a caller buffer). Either return the reply words to OCaml or shrink the reply iov to zero and document that sends are ack-only.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"MsgSendv collects the reply into a stack-local buffer that is never read — the reply payload is silently discarded (the comment suggests a caller buffer). Either return the reply words to OCaml or shrink the reply iov to zero and document that sends are ack-only.\n","text":"MsgSendv collects the reply into a stack-local buffer that is never read — the reply payload is silently discarded (the comment suggests a caller buffer). Either return the reply words to OCaml or shrink the reply iov to zero and document that sends are ack-only.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-msgsendv-reply-discarded","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-msgsendv-reply-discarded","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-msgsendv-reply-discarded"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Plain (non-acquire) read of a shared region word inside attach/config. Configuration words written at init are only safe to read after an explicit handshake (magic word / IPC) establishes happens-before. Single-owner Arena free-list walks are out of scope for this rule — they are not cross-process publication. Use Region.load_acq when the writer may still be initializing.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Plain (non-acquire) read of a shared region word inside attach/config. Configuration words written at init are only safe to read after an explicit handshake (magic word / IPC) establishes happens-before. Single-owner Arena free-list walks are out of scope for this rule — they are not cross-process publication. Use Region.load_acq when the writer may still be initializing.\n","text":"Plain (non-acquire) read of a shared region word inside attach/config. Configuration words written at init are only safe to read after an explicit handshake (magic word / IPC) establishes happens-before. Single-owner Arena free-list walks are out of scope for this rule — they are not cross-process publication. Use Region.load_acq when the writer may still be initializing.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-plain-read-of-shared-config","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-plain-read-of-shared-config","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-plain-read-of-shared-config"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Plain (non-atomic) store on the Merkle update path. Initialization stores are expected and excluded; update publishes leaf→root with no release store and no acquire on the reader side, so a cross-process reader can observe the new root with stale children (or a torn path). Use store_rel on the root and load_acq on reads, or document a quiescence requirement.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Plain (non-atomic) store on the Merkle update path. Initialization stores are expected and excluded; update publishes leaf→root with no release store and no acquire on the reader side, so a cross-process reader can observe the new root with stale children (or a torn path). Use store_rel on the root and load_acq on reads, or document a quiescence requirement.\n","text":"Plain (non-atomic) store on the Merkle update path. Initialization stores are expected and excluded; update publishes leaf→root with no release store and no acquire on the reader side, so a cross-process reader can observe the new root with stale children (or a torn path). Use store_rel on the root and load_acq on reads, or document a quiescence requirement.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-plain-store-to-shared-word","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-plain-store-to-shared-word","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-plain-store-to-shared-word"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"The copy log is an OCaml heap list consed per copied node, and the version window is a heap list too. Every Pmap.set therefore allocates on the OCaml heap in steady state — the strongest form of the \"never allocate\" claim does not hold for Pmap. Either scope the claim to Ring/Arena/Vclock/Merkle, or move the log into the region.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"The copy log is an OCaml heap list consed per copied node, and the version window is a heap list too. Every Pmap.set therefore allocates on the OCaml heap in steady state — the strongest form of the \"never allocate\" claim does not hold for Pmap. Either scope the claim to Ring/Arena/Vclock/Merkle, or move the log into the region.\n","text":"The copy log is an OCaml heap list consed per copied node, and the version window is a heap list too. Every Pmap.set therefore allocates on the OCaml heap in steady state — the strongest form of the \"never allocate\" claim does not hold for Pmap. Either scope the claim to Ring/Arena/Vclock/Merkle, or move the log into the region.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-pmap-copy-log-heap-alloc","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-pmap-copy-log-heap-alloc","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-pmap-copy-log-heap-alloc"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"MsgSendPulse fails with EAGAIN when the receiver's pulse pool is exhausted; failing hard turns backpressure (producer spin-waiting on a full ring, pulsing every retry) into a producer crash. Return the error to OCaml and let the caller rate-limit or coalesce wakeups.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"MsgSendPulse fails with EAGAIN when the receiver's pulse pool is exhausted; failing hard turns backpressure (producer spin-waiting on a full ring, pulsing every retry) into a producer crash. Return the error to OCaml and let the caller rate-limit or coalesce wakeups.\n","text":"MsgSendPulse fails with EAGAIN when the receiver's pulse pool is exhausted; failing hard turns backpressure (producer spin-waiting on a full ring, pulsing every retry) into a producer crash. Return the error to OCaml and let the caller rate-limit or coalesce wakeups.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-pulse-eagain-crashes-producer","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-pulse-eagain-crashes-producer","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-pulse-eagain-crashes-producer"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"A shared 64-bit counter is truncated to a 63-bit OCaml int. The monotone-never-wrap invariant holds for int64, but arithmetic after to_int wraps at 2^62; OCaml's (mod) then goes negative and indexing raises. Fine for realistic lifetimes, but the \"never wrapped\" claim in the docs must be qualified to 63 bits on the OCaml side.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"A shared 64-bit counter is truncated to a 63-bit OCaml int. The monotone-never-wrap invariant holds for int64, but arithmetic after to_int wraps at 2^62; OCaml's (mod) then goes negative and indexing raises. Fine for realistic lifetimes, but the \"never wrapped\" claim in the docs must be qualified to 63 bits on the OCaml side.\n","text":"A shared 64-bit counter is truncated to a 63-bit OCaml int. The monotone-never-wrap invariant holds for int64, but arithmetic after to_int wraps at 2^62; OCaml's (mod) then goes negative and indexing raises. Fine for realistic lifetimes, but the \"never wrapped\" claim in the docs must be qualified to 63 bits on the OCaml side.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-shared-counter-truncation","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-shared-counter-truncation","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-shared-counter-truncation"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"error"},"fullDescription":{"text":"shm_open create path uses O_CREAT without O_EXCL. A second creator (or a retry after a crash) opens the existing object and may ftruncate it under live peers — two logical sessions share one name. Require O_CREAT|O_EXCL for exclusive create, or open existing read-only and validate generation/size before joining.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"shm_open create path uses O_CREAT without O_EXCL. A second creator (or a retry after a crash) opens the existing object and may ftruncate it under live peers — two logical sessions share one name. Require O_CREAT|O_EXCL for exclusive create, or open existing read-only and validate generation/size before joining.\n","text":"shm_open create path uses O_CREAT without O_EXCL. A second creator (or a retry after a crash) opens the existing object and may ftruncate it under live peers — two logical sessions share one name. Require O_CREAT|O_EXCL for exclusive create, or open existing read-only and validate generation/size before joining.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-shm-create-without-excl","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-shm-create-without-excl","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-shm-create-without-excl"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"warning"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Shift amount comes straight from an OCaml value with no range check; cpu >= 64 is undefined behavior. Clamp or validate before shifting.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Shift amount comes straight from an OCaml value with no range check; cpu >= 64 is undefined behavior. Clamp or validate before shifting.\n","text":"Shift amount comes straight from an OCaml value with no range check; cpu >= 64 is undefined behavior. Clamp or validate before shifting.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-unchecked-shift-amount","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-unchecked-shift-amount","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-unchecked-shift-amount"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"error"},"fullDescription":{"text":"Region.unlink immediately followed by Region.create. Unconditional unlink of a well-known name creates two disconnected generations: old consumers retain the old mapping while the new producer binds a new object under the same path. Prefer unique session names (name + generation), O_CREAT|O_EXCL, layout version checks, publish READY last, and munmap before retry; never unlink-then-recreate while peers may still be mapped.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"Region.unlink immediately followed by Region.create. Unconditional unlink of a well-known name creates two disconnected generations: old consumers retain the old mapping while the new producer binds a new object under the same path. Prefer unique session names (name + generation), O_CREAT|O_EXCL, layout version checks, publish READY last, and munmap before retry; never unlink-then-recreate while peers may still be mapped.\n","text":"Region.unlink immediately followed by Region.create. Unconditional unlink of a well-known name creates two disconnected generations: old consumers retain the old mapping while the new producer binds a new object under the same path. Prefer unique session names (name + generation), O_CREAT|O_EXCL, layout version checks, publish READY last, and munmap before retry; never unlink-then-recreate while peers may still be mapped.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-unlink-before-create-same-name","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-unlink-before-create-same-name","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-unlink-before-create-same-name"}},{"defaultConfiguration":{"level":"note"},"fullDescription":{"text":"memset with a zero length is a no-op (and trips generic \"insecure memset\" rules). Delete it; use a real length, or the explicit pre-fault loop already present after mmap.\n"},"help":{"markdown":"memset with a zero length is a no-op (and trips generic \"insecure memset\" rules). Delete it; use a real length, or the explicit pre-fault loop already present after mmap.\n","text":"memset with a zero length is a no-op (and trips generic \"insecure memset\" rules). Delete it; use a real length, or the explicit pre-fault loop already present after mmap.\n"},"id":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-zero-length-memset","name":"tools.opengrep.qnxml-zero-length-memset","properties":{"precision":"very-high","tags":[]},"shortDescription":{"text":"Opengrep Finding: tools.opengrep.qnxml-zero-length-memset"}}],"semanticVersion":"1.10.0"}}}],"$schema":"https://docs.oasis-open.org/sarif/sarif/v2.1.0/os/schemas/sarif-schema-2.1.0.json"}